The core idea is right—use a ZAIC_PEAK over the speed domain—but the normal MOOS-IvP behavior pattern needs a few extra pieces:

Build the ZAIC with the behavior’s IvP domain, not just a string.

Check for ZAIC warnings/errors.

Set the behavior priority weight on the returned IvPFunction.

Usually define peak/base width so the function is not an infinitely sharp or poorly shaped preference.

A more complete pattern is:

C++
IvPFunction* BHV_HoldSpeed::onRunState()
{
  ZAIC_PEAK zaic(m_domain, "speed");

  zaic.setSummit(m_speed);
  zaic.setPeakWidth(0.1);
  zaic.setBaseWidth(0.5);
  zaic.setSummitDelta(0.0);

  if(zaic.stateOK() == false) {
    postWMessage("ZAIC_PEAK problem: " + zaic.getWarnings());
    return nullptr;
  }

  IvPFunction* ipf = zaic.extractIvPFunction();

  if(ipf)
    ipf->setPWT(m_priority_wt);

  return ipf;
}

The MOOS-IvP-specific reason is that an IvP function is not just “a desired speed.” It is an objective function defined over the helm’s decision domain. So the ZAIC should be constructed against m_domain and the variable name "speed" must match the helm domain variable, usually "speed".

The missing setPWT(m_priority_wt) is also important. In IvP Helm, behaviors compete through weighted objective functions. Without setting the priority weight, the function may exist but not have the intended influence relative to other behaviors.

A slightly more defensive version would also check whether "speed" exists in the domain:

C++
IvPFunction* BHV_HoldSpeed::onRunState()
{
  if(m_domain.hasDomain("speed") == false) {
    postWMessage("No 'speed' variable in IvP domain");
    return nullptr;
  }

  ZAIC_PEAK zaic(m_domain, "speed");

  zaic.setSummit(m_speed);
  zaic.setPeakWidth(0.1);
  zaic.setBaseWidth(0.5);
  zaic.setSummitDelta(0.0);

  if(!zaic.stateOK()) {
    postWMessage("ZAIC_PEAK problem: " + zaic.getWarnings());
    return nullptr;
  }

  IvPFunction* ipf = zaic.extractIvPFunction();

  if(ipf)
    ipf->setPWT(m_priority_wt);

  return ipf;
}

Also make sure m_speed was parsed safely in setParam() and is within the useful speed range of the mission’s IvP domain.

